 even district, and most raters call this a flip with markets agreeing at roughly 81% for Rutinel. The lone poll, from a Republican-leaning pollster, shows Evans up two, so the race stays competitive rather than moving further toward Democrats.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling and whether Evans's cash advantage ($3.9M on hand) can offset the Democratic outside-spending wave.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Evans (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 8th District is a pure battleground with no partisan lean, and first-term Republican Gabe Evans is defending the seat against Democrat Manny Rutinel in what raters view as a favorable year for Democrats. Nine of the major ratings call the race a Democratic flip, ranging from Tossup to Likely D, and prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket, and PredictIt all price Rutinel near 81%. Rutinel has raised $4.3M and benefits from about $3.5M in outside support, while Evans holds a large cash-on-hand edge of $3.9M. The only public poll, from a Republican-leaning firm in mid-September, shows Evans narrowly ahead, and headlines describe the race as tied or narrowly favoring Evans. The forecast lands on a modest Democratic edge with wide uncertainty, consistent with a Lean D rating in a genuinely competitive district.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7882, "margin": 4.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7652, "margin": 4.0}]}